content.text:
The evidence points to one fault: the frontend-to-profile path is cut while the profile process stays healthy. I'm finalizing the answer from the queries already run.

```json
{
  "root_causes": [
    {
      "service": "profile",
      "fault_kind": "network_partition",
      "evidence": [
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT CASE WHEN duration<1e6 THEN '<1ms' WHEN duration<1e9 THEN '1ms-1s' ELSE '>=1s' END bucket, attr_status_code, count(*) n FROM abnormal_traces WHERE span_name='profile.Profile/GetProfiles' GROUP BY ALL ORDER BY 1,2", "claim": "Frontend's GetProfiles calls to profile almost all fail in under 1ms; only 2 take over a second."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT 'abn' src, service_name, count(*) n FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='profile' GROUP BY ALL UNION ALL SELECT 'norm', service_name, count(*) FROM normal_traces WHERE service_name='profile' GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "Profile server spans fall from 1342 in the normal period to 2 in the abnormal period."},
        {"kind": "metric", "sql": "SELECT 'abn' src, attr_k8s_pod_name pod, metric, min(time) t0, max(time) t1, count(*) n, round(min(value),1) mn, round(max(value),1) mx FROM abnormal_metrics_sum WHERE attr_k8s_pod_name ILIKE 'profile-%' AND metric ILIKE '%uptime%' GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "Profile pod uptime climbs continuously through the abnormal window, so the pod never restarted."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT p.span_name parent, p.attr_http_response_status_code code, c.span_name child, c.attr_status_code child_status, count(*) n FROM abnormal_traces p JOIN abnormal_traces c ON c.trace_id=p.trace_id AND c.parent_span_id=p.span_id WHERE p.span_name IN ('HTTP /hotels','HTTP /recommendations') AND c.span_name='profile.Profile/GetProfiles' GROUP BY ALL", "claim": "Frontend's /hotels and /recommendations 500 responses sit on top of failed profile GetProfiles calls."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "propagation": [
    {"from": "profile", "to": "frontend",
     "evidence": [{"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT date_trunc('minute', time) m, sum(CASE WHEN span_name='profile.Profile/GetProfiles' AND attr_status_code='Error' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) prof_err, sum(CASE WHEN span_name='HTTP /hotels' AND attr_status_code='Error' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) hotels_err FROM abnormal_traces GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1", "claim": "Every minute, profile call failures at frontend track frontend /hotels HTTP 500 errors that users see."}]}
  ]
}
```

The degradation comes from one fault: the path from `frontend` to `profile` is cut, and this is best labelled `network_partition`. `profile` stops receiving requests, but its pod stays up and does not restart. The fault has no direct network telemetry to confirm it, so the kind is my best reading rather than a certainty.

- **Affected endpoints:** `HTTP /hotels` (876 errors, all 500) and `HTTP /recommendations` (494 errors, all 500) at `frontend`. Both sit on top of failed `profile.Profile/GetProfiles` calls. Other frontend endpoints (`/cinema`, `/museums`, `/reservation`) are unaffected.
- **Why this kind:** `profile`'s server spans fell from 1342 to 2, and its CPU dropped to near idle. Its uptime is continuous, so there was no restart. Its metrics keep reporting, so the pod was not gone. Almost all failing calls return in under 1 ms, with only 2 slower than a second. That points to requests being refused at the connection level rather than hanging on timeouts.
- **Ruled out:** a `dns_lookup_failed` at `frontend` fits the fast failures about as well. I chose `network_partition` because the rare long failures look more like timeouts on a cut connection, and because the fault sits on the `profile` side for propagation. Telling these apart needs per-connection network metrics, which this case does not have.
- **Other services:** the other services show no error increase, and the survey's large metric shifts on `memcached-profile-1`, `mongodb-profile` and `profile` look like knock-on effects of the lower request rate.
- **Propagation:** the single edge is `profile` → `frontend`. `frontend` is the user-facing entry tier, so the chain ends there.

